WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.140
What makes a grammar ambiguous?

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.620
One word must have two

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.440
different parse trees, equivalently two

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.220
different leftmost derivations. Two arbitrary

5
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.280
substitution orders are not enough,

6
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.120
because they may simply construct

7
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.260
the same tree in a

8
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.000
different order.

9
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.500
Consider E becoming E plus

10
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.020
E, E times E, a

11
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.500
parenthesized E, or a. The

12
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.120
word a plus a times

13
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.500
a can be grouped as

14
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.160
a plus the product, or

15
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.440
as a sum multiplied by

16
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.480
a. Both structures are permitted

17
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.820
because the grammar does not

18
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.780
encode precedence.

19
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.120
For the first structure, expand

20
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.560
the root using addition. Finish

21
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:40.960
the left E as a,

22
00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.000
then expand the right E

23
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.680
into a product and finish

24
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.080
its two operands. The leftmost

25
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.140
derivation therefore begins with E

26
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.980
plus E and ends with

27
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.800
a plus a times a.

28
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.780
For the second structure, expand

29
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.540
the root using multiplication. Its

30
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.060
left child becomes a sum,

31
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.040
and the leftmost variable is

32
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.120
always completed before any variable

33
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.280
to its right. This derivation

34
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.080
reaches the same terminal word

35
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.620
through a different root production,

36
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.460
guaranteeing a genuinely different tree.

37
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.640
The two trees differ at

38
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.140
their roots, so they are

39
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.740
not just two schedules for

40
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.120
expanding one structure. Both yields

41
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.100
read a plus a times

42
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.300
a from left to right,

43
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.360
even though the implicit groupings

44
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.980
differ. That single witness proves

45
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.200
the grammar ambiguous; we do

46
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.620
not need every word to

47
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.260
be ambiguous.

48
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.420
To see the wrong argument,

49
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.640
take the simple grammar S

50
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.840
becomes A B, with A

51
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.640
becoming a and B becoming

52
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.120
b. Expanding A before B

53
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.580
or B before A gives

54
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.180
two derivations of a b.

55
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:47.840
There is still only one

56
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.840
parse tree, so those derivations

57
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:50.900
do not prove ambiguity.

58
00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.700
An expression grammar can enforce

59
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.800
precedence by using separate levels

60
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.620
for expressions, terms and factors.

61
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.240
Addition combines expressions and terms,

62
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.480
while multiplication stays inside terms,

63
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.820
and factors are atoms or

64
00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.420
parenthesized expressions. This prevents the

65
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.240
competing root multiplication for an

66
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.120
unparenthesized sum such as our

67
00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:10.420
witness.

68
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.640
Under that grammar, multiplication binds

69
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.320
more tightly than addition. Repeated

70
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.560
left-recursive additions also fix their

71
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.440
grouping rather than creating arbitrary

72
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.720
associative parse trees. If you

73
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:23.800
are asked for a leftmost

74
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.180
derivation, replace the leftmost remaining

75
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.660
variable at every step and

76
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.280
show the whole requested word.

77
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.520
Ambiguity belongs to a particular

78
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.080
grammar. A language is inherently

79
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.380
ambiguous only when every grammar

80
00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.980
generating it is ambiguous, which

81
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.460
is a much stronger statement.

82
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.500
Showing that this expression grammar

83
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.140
is ambiguous does not prove

84
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.760
inherent ambiguity, since the precedence

85
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.540
grammar generates the same language

86
00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.480
without that defect.

87
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.300
Things to take note of.

88
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.660
Name one terminal witness and

89
00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.180
give two different trees or

90
00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.600
two distinct leftmost derivations for

91
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.480
it. Check that their yields

92
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.100
agree and their structures differ;

93
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.100
merely changing the order of

94
00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.640
independent substitutions proves nothing about

95
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:07.180
ambiguity.
